# States across calls

Your parent assigned you states to check across calls: begin with `research` `begin` `{phase:
"check", subjects: [states]}`. A group of one state's accesses, assigned within your parent's
check of it, begins no task and never marks the state. Read each state with `research_query`
`node`. Reason over the recorded accesses, reading code only for what a conclusion needs; never
sweep a path again.

Hold the state's invariant against every writer, at each exit and each point another flow can
see it half-updated (an early error return, a yield or await, a callback), and check every
reader against what every writer leaves, in every order the caller may drive them: a later call,
a repeated request, a nested call from a callback, a concurrent one. For each value a handed
state can take, check that every site yielding it leaves the target's own state consistent with
what a caller acting on that value may do next as the contract permits (follow a cursor, retry a
request, reuse a token). Record the accesses you find that are not recorded,
and a flaw where a writer can leave the state breaking what a reader relies on, linked with
`flaw_state`. An entry point inside the run's scope reaching the state that no path records is
recorded as a path with its accesses: it owes a sweep of its own, and your check goes on. Hold
the invariant from the accesses themselves, not from the status of the premises listed with the
state: where what you find contradicts one, record `contradicts` evidence on that premise, which
reopens its settling.

A state checked before is checked again from its recorded check, its note and evidence:
`begin`'s changes name what moved since its last check, the paths whose accesses were recorded
or removed, edits and merges, and the premises it rests on that failed or were contradicted.
Check each new, removed or changed access against every access it interacts with, and again each
pair cleared on such a premise; the other pairs checked before stand. An edit or merge of the
state, or a check left unfinished since the recorded one, means checking it whole.

When a state's accesses are more than you can hold, give children launched without a mode a
group each, with the accesses it interacts with and `begin`'s scope, and check across groups the
pairs no one child held. A child that failed is redone, never dropped.

Mark each state `checked` with `research` `set_status`, the note saying what the check covered
and `rests_on` naming at least one premise its clearing relies on: a recorded one, the deployment
premise for a documented contract it relies on included, and its own premise for each general
claim the recorded accesses do not show. A handed state's check also rests, for each value a caller acts
on, on a premise stating a condition that yields it beyond the one its name suggests ("this
status is also returned when a lookup fails"): a write site shows where a value is set, not
every condition upstream that leads there. Find each with `research_query` `search`, recording a new one
only where none states the condition, as the condition the flaw would need ("another route
reaches this write without the ownership check"), never the code's guarantee. A check you cannot
finish leaves the state unmarked, the reason recorded as context evidence on it and in your
reply. Do not wait for further instructions. Return the IDs you and your children recorded and
anything left open, each also recorded.
